Targeted workaround for a GCC -Wfree-nonheap-object false positive.

https://github.com/fluent/fluent-bit/issues/10510 reports the same two warnings
against 4.0.3; auto-closed "not planned" by stale-bot with no fix, discussion, or
linked PR, and still reproduces unmodified in 5.1.1.

Diagnostic (GCC 16.2.0, -O2, no LTO):

  In function 'mk_mem_free',
      inlined from 'mk_stream_release' at mk_stream.h:255:9,
      inlined from 'mk_http_request_free' at mk_http.c:1521:9:
  mk_core/mk_memory.h:102:5: warning: 'free' called on pointer 'sr' with
  nonzero offset 32 [-Wfree-nonheap-object]

  In function 'mk_mem_free',
      inlined from 'mk_stream_release' at mk_stream.h:255:9,
      inlined from 'dummy_mk_http_request_init' at
        flb_http_server_http1.c:53:9:
  mk_core/mk_memory.h:102:5: warning: 'free' called on pointer 'request' with
  nonzero offset 32 [-Wfree-nonheap-object]

Both trace to the same free(ptr) inside mk_stream_release()'s
"if (stream->dynamic == MK_TRUE) mk_mem_free(stream);" guard -- not to
mk_http_request_free_list()'s separate "request != &cs->sr_fixed" guard (that
one is correct and untouched; cs->request_list only ever holds
&cs->sr_fixed._head in this codebase).

struct mk_stream's "dynamic" flag is the actual heap/embedded distinction
(mk_stream_set()): dynamic=MK_TRUE only via mk_stream_set(NULL, ...), which
mk_stream.h's own comment says only Monkey's CGI plugin does. That plugin is
absent from Fluent Bit's build (zero plugins/cgi references in build.ninja),
so at the two warned call sites (&sr->stream, &request->stream -- both fields
embedded in a caller-owned struct mk_http_request) dynamic is always
MK_FALSE and mk_mem_free(stream) is unreachable: the runtime guard is
correct, GCC's interprocedural points-to analysis just doesn't track
"dynamic" across the inline, so it warns as if free() could see an interior
pointer.

Reproduces identically on every GCC installed on this host, 11.5.0 through
16.2.0; does not reproduce under Clang 22.1.8 compiling the same translation
unit with equivalent flags. Scoped to __GNUC__ >= 11, the verified-affected
range's lower bound (no earlier GCC was available to test, so no claim is
made below 11): Clang also defines __GNUC__, but pins it at 4 for
compatibility, so it takes the unchanged "static inline" branch too.

mk_stream_release() has three call sites in code Fluent Bit actually builds:
mk_http.c:1521, flb_http_server_http1.c:53 (both warned), and
mk_stream.c:337 (mk_channel_clean(), not warned -- there stream->dynamic is
genuinely mixed, since mk_channel_clean() iterates a list that can hold both
kinds of stream, so the guard does real work there and GCC evidently didn't
inline that call). "noinline" stops GCC inlining mk_stream_release() at ALL
of these sites, not only the two that currently warn -- it does not change
what runs at any of them; mk_channel_clean()'s guard behaves identically
either way. "inline" must be dropped for "noinline" to apply (GCC rejects the
combination: "inline function ... given attribute 'noinline'
[-Wattributes]"); "unused" is added back so a translation unit that includes
this header without calling the function doesn't get -Wunused-function on
the resulting plain "static" definition (the exemption "static inline"
provided implicitly is lost with "inline" gone).

--- a/lib/monkey/include/monkey/mk_stream.h
+++ b/lib/monkey/include/monkey/mk_stream.h
@@ -233,7 +233,21 @@
 }


-static inline void mk_stream_release(struct mk_stream *stream)
+/* Compiler-specific workaround for a GCC false positive:
+ * https://github.com/fluent/fluent-bit/issues/10510. GCC misreads the
+ * "dynamic == MK_TRUE" guard below when this is inlined into a caller
+ * passing an embedded (non-heap) struct mk_stream, and warns
+ * -Wfree-nonheap-object on mk_mem_free(stream) as if it saw an interior
+ * pointer. Blocking inlining silences it without changing the guard.
+ * Verified needed on GCC 11.5.0-16.2.0; Clang defines __GNUC__ too but
+ * pins it at 4, so it takes the unchanged branch below.
+ */
+#if __GNUC__ >= 11
+static __attribute__((noinline, unused))
+void mk_stream_release(struct mk_stream *stream)
+#else
+static inline void mk_stream_release(struct mk_stream *stream)
+#endif
 {
     struct mk_list *tmp;
     struct mk_list *head;
